

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good — that's the shape I'd have recommended. Next question, then I'll lay out approaches.

  [1mThe fork:[0m "it should persist" has two very different meanings in a browser, and they differ in security posture rather
  than in code volume.

  - [1m[38;5;153msessionStorage[39m, cleared on logout[0m — the ID survives page navigation and reloads, and dies when the tab closes. Buys
    you "works across the app" with the shortest exposure window. Costs: reopening the browser means the user is
    unidentified again until they log in, which is correct behavior but might not be what you meant by persist.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, cleared on logout[0m — survives browser restarts until explicitly cleared. Buys you continuity across
    days. Costs: a user identifier sits on disk indefinitely; on a shared machine, a crash or a skipped logout leaves
    the previous person's ID readable by any script on the origin. This is the one that needs a deliberate yes.
  - [1mIn-memory only, no browser storage[0m — a module holds it for the page's lifetime. Buys you zero at-rest exposure.
    Costs: a reload loses it, so it doesn't really satisfy "persists" — I'm listing it mainly so the comparison is
    honest.

  Storage choice is cheap to change later — it'll sit behind one module either way. What's [3mnot[0m cheap is anything we
  build assuming the ID is always present: if you pick [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m, every consumer has to handle "no ID yet," and
  retrofitting that is the expensive direction.

  [1mMy recommendation: [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m, cleared on logout.[0m The server reissues the ID at each login, so durable on-disk
  storage buys continuity you don't need for tracking and costs you a real exposure window on shared devices.
